WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:02.568
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-winning

00:00:02.568 --> 00:00:04.403
and Grammy-nominated
comedian and writer

00:00:04.403 --> 00:00:06.740
you know from her work
on "Black-ish," "The Upshaws,"

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:08.507
and "Curb Your Enthusiasm."

00:00:08.507 --> 00:00:10.343
Her scripted
comedy podcast series

00:00:10.343 --> 00:00:13.146
"Trust Fall" is available
November 30th on Audible.

00:00:13.146 --> 00:00:14.480
Please welcome back
to the show our friend

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.215
Wanda Sykes,
everybody!

00:00:16.215 --> 00:00:18.484
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:18.484 --> 00:00:20.586
[ Mid-tempo music plays ]

00:00:20.586 --> 00:00:35.835
♪♪

00:00:35.835 --> 00:00:38.638
Welcome back, Wanda!
-Good to be back.

00:00:38.638 --> 00:00:41.808
-Congratulations on
your Grammy nomination.

00:00:41.808 --> 00:00:44.477
-Why, thank you.
-Yes, very, very exciting.

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:46.345
-It's my first one.
-It's your first

00:00:46.345 --> 00:00:48.013
Grammy nomination?
-First Grammy nomination.

00:00:48.013 --> 00:00:50.516
-That's very exciting.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:50.516 --> 00:00:52.285
-I know.
I feel like, you know,

00:00:52.285 --> 00:00:55.188
I'm like a rock star now.
You know?

00:00:55.188 --> 00:00:57.023
-Your twins are 14?
-Yes.

00:00:57.023 --> 00:00:58.491
-I would think
they would be on the cusp

00:00:58.491 --> 00:01:00.360
of wanting to go to the Grammys.
Do they want to go?

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.462
-Of course they want to go.
-Are you going to let them go?

00:01:02.462 --> 00:01:04.097
-Hell, no.
-Okay.

00:01:04.097 --> 00:01:05.598
[ Laughter ]
Have you told them this?

00:01:05.598 --> 00:01:07.967
-Um, I-I think
they see it on my face.

00:01:07.967 --> 00:01:09.168
-Gotcha.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:09.168 --> 00:01:10.870
-They're like,
"Oh, that's so cool!

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:13.005
You're going to the Grammys?"

00:01:13.005 --> 00:01:16.643
And I just do this -- "Uh-huh."
[ Laughter ]

00:01:16.643 --> 00:01:18.745
See, that says...
-Yeah, yeah.

00:01:18.745 --> 00:01:21.447
-..."You're not going," right?
They don't even have to ask.

00:01:21.447 --> 00:01:24.517
-If you did bring them, would
you want to be seen with them?

00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:26.986
-Would I want to be
seen with them?

00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:28.320
-Or would you want to
put some distance

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:29.655
between you and your daught--
-No.

00:01:29.655 --> 00:01:32.692
They will put distance
between me.

00:01:32.692 --> 00:01:35.662
Yeah, like, Olivia will go try
to sit with Beyoncé

00:01:35.662 --> 00:01:37.029
or something.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:01:37.029 --> 00:01:38.998
-They would just leave me.
-Yeah. You can't bring them.

00:01:38.998 --> 00:01:41.066
You absolutely can't.
-They would dust me.

00:01:41.066 --> 00:01:44.637
-Now, at 14, obviously, they're
making choices for themselves.

00:01:44.637 --> 00:01:46.572
What are they watching?
Like, are there things

00:01:46.572 --> 00:01:48.407
they watch
that is confusing to you?

00:01:48.407 --> 00:01:50.676
-I have no idea
what they're watching.

00:01:50.676 --> 00:01:55.415
I walk by, and they --
And it's all, like, YouTube.

00:01:55.415 --> 00:01:58.050
They watch stuff on YouTube,
and it's -- usually,

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:00.953
they're watching
other people do stuff.

00:02:00.953 --> 00:02:04.690
[ Laughter ]
Like, they spend hours

00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:07.926
watching this guy
play a video game.

00:02:07.926 --> 00:02:10.063
[ Laughter ]
It makes no sense.

00:02:10.063 --> 00:02:13.432
Like, "Why
are y'all watching this?"

00:02:13.432 --> 00:02:15.268
And it was like,
"Well, it's kind of cool."

00:02:15.268 --> 00:02:18.438
I'm like, "So, you're telling me
you're too damn lazy

00:02:18.438 --> 00:02:21.073
to play this game yourself,

00:02:21.073 --> 00:02:24.377
so you'd rather watch
somebody play the game for you?"

00:02:24.377 --> 00:02:27.279
-It's so funny, too,
back in my day, it'd be like,

00:02:27.279 --> 00:02:29.415
"You know, when I was young,
I played my own video game."

00:02:29.415 --> 00:02:30.850
[ Laughter ]

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:32.285
I can't believe
that it's come to a point,

00:02:32.285 --> 00:02:34.053
the thing my dad
was mad at me about,

00:02:34.053 --> 00:02:35.788
I'm like, "You have no idea
how much worse it gets."

00:02:35.788 --> 00:02:37.890
-Exactly. [ Laughs ]
-So much worse.

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:40.826
Now, your wife is French.
-Yes.

00:02:40.826 --> 00:02:44.330
-And do you feel like
your kids are multicultural?

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:45.899
And are there times
where you can see

00:02:45.899 --> 00:02:48.101
the French side of them
coming out more?

00:02:48.101 --> 00:02:51.271
-My kids are barely American,
they're so French.

00:02:51.271 --> 00:02:53.606
-And they grew up here.
-So French.

00:02:53.606 --> 00:02:55.107
-Yeah.
-Yes.

00:02:55.107 --> 00:02:56.842
-And what part of them --
Where do you see the Frenchness

00:02:56.842 --> 00:02:59.712
the most?
-Uh, just, like, the --

00:02:59.712 --> 00:03:03.883
Well, of course, language
and, uh, humor.

00:03:03.883 --> 00:03:08.287
My son has the same type of
humor as my wife.

00:03:08.287 --> 00:03:09.755
-Yeah.
-Which means, a lot of it,

00:03:09.755 --> 00:03:11.557
I don't get.
-Yeah.

00:03:11.557 --> 00:03:12.925
[ Laughter ]

00:03:12.925 --> 00:03:14.627
-I will say --
and I hope your wife

00:03:14.627 --> 00:03:16.095
doesn't take this
the wrong way --

00:03:16.095 --> 00:03:17.596
It's very heartbreaking to me

00:03:17.596 --> 00:03:20.299
that your kids
have Wanda Sykes as a mom,

00:03:20.299 --> 00:03:22.268
and they are getting
a French sense of humor.

00:03:22.268 --> 00:03:26.005
[ Laughter ]

00:03:26.005 --> 00:03:29.208
That is a terrib--
That broke terribly for them.

00:03:29.208 --> 00:03:31.210
[ Laughter ]

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:33.579
[ Applause ]

00:03:33.579 --> 00:03:37.483
But you're like, "They've
learned cuisine from me --

00:03:37.483 --> 00:03:38.918
Wanda Sykes," yeah.
-Right. Exactly.

00:03:38.918 --> 00:03:41.687
You know what? You're right.
Yeah, exactly, exactly, yeah.

00:03:41.687 --> 00:03:43.122
-Yeah, so, they're French.

00:03:43.122 --> 00:03:44.924
Does that mean
they don't do American holidays?

00:03:44.924 --> 00:03:46.993
Like, what are they excited
about Thanksgiving?

00:03:46.993 --> 00:03:50.496
-They're excited about it,
but they're going to France.

00:03:50.496 --> 00:03:51.931
-Okay.
-Yeah.

00:03:51.931 --> 00:03:53.332
-Are you going to France?
-No.

00:03:53.332 --> 00:03:54.466
-Okay.
-No, no.

00:03:54.466 --> 00:03:55.968
It's Thanksgiving. Yeah.

00:03:55.968 --> 00:03:58.137
[ Laughter ]

00:03:58.137 --> 00:04:00.173
I'm going to my parents' house.
-That's great.

00:04:00.173 --> 00:04:02.574
So you go to your parents' house
and they just go --

00:04:02.574 --> 00:04:03.909
-They going to France.

00:04:03.909 --> 00:04:05.711
-They go off with your wife
to France.

00:04:05.711 --> 00:04:07.780
-I guess that's because
they have school off,

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:10.283
and it makes sense.
-Exactly, exactly, exactly.

00:04:10.283 --> 00:04:11.817
-And do they not -- I mean,
obviously,

00:04:11.817 --> 00:04:13.485
they've had Thanksgiving.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:04:13.485 --> 00:04:14.920
-They just don't care about it
that much?

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:17.256
-Well, it's not --
No, they they do enjoy it.

00:04:17.256 --> 00:04:20.726
But they, you know,
they love France

00:04:20.726 --> 00:04:23.095
a little bit more
than eating some turkey.

00:04:23.095 --> 00:04:24.664
They're like, "Ugh."
-Yeah, right, that's true.

00:04:24.664 --> 00:04:26.698
-[ French accent ] Turkey --
I don't know.

00:04:26.698 --> 00:04:28.467
Non, merci.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:28.467 --> 00:04:31.136
-Now, Wanda,
you're doing something here

00:04:31.136 --> 00:04:36.876
that a 14-year-old --
-It's a -- It's not a chicken.

00:04:36.876 --> 00:04:38.744
N'est pas poulet.

00:04:38.744 --> 00:04:41.847
[ Speaking French ]

00:04:41.847 --> 00:04:45.084
Turkey. Um, um...

00:04:45.084 --> 00:04:46.518
Non, merci.

00:04:46.518 --> 00:04:47.853
-Oh, my God,
if they're like that,

00:04:47.853 --> 00:04:49.922
you must be so thankful
they're going to France.

00:04:49.922 --> 00:04:52.725
[ Laughter ]

00:04:52.725 --> 00:04:55.962
So, what is -- Growing up
in your house,

00:04:55.962 --> 00:04:57.696
what does -- you're going
to see your parents.

00:04:57.696 --> 00:05:00.565
So, what's a Sykes family
Thanksgiving like?

00:05:00.565 --> 00:05:01.901
Is it a lot of people?

00:05:01.901 --> 00:05:03.903
-[ Normal voice ] It used to be
a lot of people.

00:05:03.903 --> 00:05:06.372
Now it's -- you know,
it'll be my parents

00:05:06.372 --> 00:05:12.445
and my brother and sister-in-law
and, you know, my nephew.

00:05:12.445 --> 00:05:15.281
So, yeah, it's like 8 of us,
10 of us, sometimes.

00:05:15.281 --> 00:05:17.950
-Good.
-And it's always some strays.

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:20.552
You got to bring in
a stray or two.

00:05:20.552 --> 00:05:22.021
-Yeah.
-Yeah, so...

00:05:22.021 --> 00:05:24.157
-It kind of cuts the tension
to have a couple outsiders.

00:05:24.157 --> 00:05:26.892
-It does.
If they're all insiders,

00:05:26.892 --> 00:05:28.694
then it's the family's fault
when you start having

00:05:28.694 --> 00:05:30.262
a bad time.
-Thank you, thank you,

00:05:30.262 --> 00:05:32.231
thank you, thank you.
-You got to have

00:05:32.231 --> 00:05:33.799
a weird outsider
to be like,

00:05:33.799 --> 00:05:35.668
"This would have been fine
if Weird Ken wasn't here."

00:05:35.668 --> 00:05:39.305
-Yeah, and it keeps everybody
on their best behavior.

00:05:39.305 --> 00:05:41.073
-Yeah.
-'Cause it's like, you know,

00:05:41.073 --> 00:05:43.175
if you start talking about
some family stuff

00:05:43.175 --> 00:05:46.846
and then it gets a little deep,
you go, [ Clears throat ]

00:05:46.846 --> 00:05:49.081
[ Laughter ]

00:05:49.081 --> 00:05:51.250
"We got a stray,
we got a stray."

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:52.852
-Strays are very helpful.
-Yeah.

00:05:52.852 --> 00:05:55.421
-"Let's not go there right now.
Let's --" Yeah.

00:05:55.421 --> 00:05:57.690
-I think we're both thankful
of the fact

00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:00.026
that both the strikes are over.
I know you're a member of both.

00:06:00.026 --> 00:06:01.360
-Thank you. Yes.
-Both guilds.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:02.995
-Absolutely.
-Did you go out?

00:06:02.995 --> 00:06:04.330
Did you picket
during the strikes?

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:06.164
-I was out there picketing.

00:06:06.164 --> 00:06:09.735
And I was in L.A., too,
you know?

00:06:09.735 --> 00:06:12.738
It was just too daggone hot
to be out there.

00:06:12.738 --> 00:06:17.175
I was like, "Walking with
a sign, we still doing this?

00:06:17.175 --> 00:06:20.579
Feels like we should have
some better options than this.

00:06:20.579 --> 00:06:23.415
Is this still effective?"
-It's so funny, yeah.

00:06:23.415 --> 00:06:25.251
You're right, technology
has not broken through.

00:06:25.251 --> 00:06:28.554
-Exactly. Like, how about
a strongly worded e-mail?

00:06:28.554 --> 00:06:30.056
How about that?
[ Laughter ]

00:06:30.056 --> 00:06:32.624
They seem to get the job done.
-Yeah.

00:06:32.624 --> 00:06:34.660
-You know,
I don't understand this.

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:36.762
And then, you know, when --
But you know,

00:06:36.762 --> 00:06:38.597
'cause you were out there, too,
and you know.

00:06:38.597 --> 00:06:42.100
And people, still they're
our union brothers and sisters,

00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:43.769
but they don't --
they want a selfie, right?

00:06:43.769 --> 00:06:45.371
You were taking
a lot of selfies.

00:06:45.371 --> 00:06:46.539
-They want to take
a lot of pictures.

00:06:46.539 --> 00:06:48.341
-A lot of pictures.
-And they want it.

00:06:48.341 --> 00:06:49.742
And then people from the guild
always want to get

00:06:49.742 --> 00:06:52.111
a sound bite from you.
-Right, exactly. Yeah.

00:06:52.111 --> 00:06:54.213
-And the thing is,
when I'm on strike,

00:06:54.213 --> 00:06:55.547
I don't have my hair
and makeup people,

00:06:55.547 --> 00:06:57.449
so it's a pretty
bad-looking scene.

00:06:57.449 --> 00:06:59.452
-Right, right.
-I think, a lot of time,

00:06:59.452 --> 00:07:01.620
I had a very hurtful thing
that people would take selfies,

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:03.756
and then I'd watch them
look at it and just delete it

00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:05.357
when they saw
what I looked like.

00:07:05.357 --> 00:07:07.660
[ Laughter ]
When did you get your SAG card?

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:10.996
What was your first acting job
where you joined the union?

00:07:10.996 --> 00:07:14.566
-"Nutty Professor II."
-Wow! "Nutty Professor II."

00:07:14.566 --> 00:07:16.435
-"Nutty Professor II,"
I got my SAG card.

00:07:16.435 --> 00:07:18.504
Yeah, that was with, uh --

00:07:18.504 --> 00:07:21.641
That was Eddie Murphy,
and I played Glodine.

00:07:21.641 --> 00:07:23.476
I was a -- Was it
Glodine was her name?

00:07:23.476 --> 00:07:27.380
Wait, I was the hairdresser.

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:29.715
-And did you get to work
with multiple Eddies?

00:07:29.715 --> 00:07:33.685
-Yeah, I got to work with
with Mama Klump

00:07:33.685 --> 00:07:36.689
and then the grandmother.
And you know, actually,

00:07:36.689 --> 00:07:39.257
the father
came by the shop, too.

00:07:39.257 --> 00:07:40.693
-Tour de force.
-So, I mean --

00:07:40.693 --> 00:07:42.628
-I think, one of the greatest
acting performances of all time.

00:07:42.628 --> 00:07:44.697
-Yes, yes, yes.
-Eddie is unbelievable.

00:07:44.697 --> 00:07:46.532
-Eddie is amazing. Amazing.

00:07:46.532 --> 00:07:48.701
And I say that I got
to work with him,

00:07:48.701 --> 00:07:50.503
but, basically, I worked
with a bunch of tennis balls.

00:07:50.503 --> 00:07:52.338
That's what I did.
-Right,

00:07:52.338 --> 00:07:54.373
'cause they had to CGI him.
-Yes, yes.

00:07:54.373 --> 00:07:56.408
By the way, it shows
what a good actor you are.

00:07:56.408 --> 00:07:58.177
You could never have known
they were tennis balls.

00:07:58.177 --> 00:07:59.512
-Yeah.
-I have a lot more to ask about.

00:07:59.512 --> 00:08:01.514
We'll be right back
with more Wanda after this.

